    "q-166222": {
      "dom_id": "question-166222",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193298/assets/19d0c50eff5613451c833c00e2d151b93ca5d7e490366fc811d6730e383227de.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above (not drawn to scale). Estimate the area $A$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A\\,≈\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193298/assets/e13444351bb092ced6fc788ae921622f154a267e9544e928700736a53a259fc6.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.75$ | $3$ | $1.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.75+3+1.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =8.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.25\\end{aligned}\n$$",
